  "attribution": "Recomputed dev-scan benign success is serial_gate 0.5455 and joint_commit 0.9091, both with 0 reconstruction violations. Deterministic schedules do not show that gap: both are 0.9000 with the same decisions and 0 violations. Seed 0 joint_commit misses only u_test; serial_gate misses u_agg, u_agg2, u_agg3, u_pair, u_pair2, u_pair3. Every task's max candidate.utility is 1.0, equal across tasks: True. utility_greedy_reorder therefore sorts by task name and runs the serial gate; its dev benign success is 0.9091. lookahead_2 dev benign success is 0.5455. exact_type_ban dev benign success is 0.9091. exact_subset on dev: not run: flattened schedule has 17 items, above the 16-item cap. Shuffling each dev multiset with Random(seed) moves serial-gate benign success from 0.5455 to 0.9091 (50/55). Every shuffled seed misses only u_test and has 0 violations, so those orders match joint_commit utility with the serial gate and no type ban. utility_greedy_reorder matches joint_commit utility within 0.01 with 0 violations on both splits. lookahead_2 does not: on the dev scan it stays with the serial gate, because a conflicting next task has the same utility 1, the two-step sum ties, and the tie keeps the current safe release. Ordinary priority planning plus the existing gate closes the utility gap. The closure is an order, not a new predicate: every max utility is 1, so the reorder is alphabetical and puts u_test last, after aggregates and summaries are already held. exact_subset on the 15-item deterministic schedules also reaches 0.9000, but its decisions differ because it refuses p_joint instead of releasing that probe's aggregate and summary. It was not run on the 17-item dev sessions. Safety gain versus the serial gate is 0. Diagnosis verdict STOP. The historical X1 experiment remains STOP and is not a PASS.",
